# User-Facing Terminology

The following words should appear in the interface.

## Journey

A collection of memories from one holiday or experience.

Examples:

* Japan 2028
* New York 2026
* Summer in Sweden

---

## Memory Card

The introduction shown before every journey.

Contains:

* title
* location
* dates
* favourite quote
* personal story

Every journey begins here.

---

## Gallery

The idle screen.

The Gallery patiently waits for the next journey.

It is never referred to as "Home."

---

## Memories

The individual experiences contained within a journey.

Internally these may be implemented as media objects or moments, but users should simply experience them as memories.

---

## Favourite Quote

A short sentence that captures the feeling of a journey.

Examples:

> "The holiday where Ira decided every pigeon was her best friend."

> "We laughed harder than we slept."

This field is optional.

---

# Internal Engineering Terminology

The codebase may use more technical language.

Recommended internal model:

```text
Journey

└── Moment

    ├── Memory Card
    ├── Photo
    ├── Live Photo
    ├── Video (future)
    ├── Ticket (future)
    └── Audio (future)
```

The word **Moment** is an implementation concept and should rarely appear in the user interface.

---

# Writing Style

## Prefer

Simple language.

Example:

> Tap a travel magnet to relive a journey.

Instead of:

> Scan an NFC tag to launch an album.

---

## Prefer

Natural sentences.

Example:

> Journey imported successfully.

Instead of:

> Import operation completed successfully.

---

## Prefer

Warm confirmation.

Example:

> Your journey is ready.

Instead of:

> Import complete.

---

# Empty States

Empty states should invite rather than apologise.

## Gallery

> **Your gallery is waiting.**

> Add your first journey to begin building your family memory collection.

---

## No Journeys

> No journeys yet.

> Add your first journey and start creating lasting memories.

---

## No Memories

> This journey is waiting for its first memories.

---

# NFC Interaction

When a journey is recognised:

Avoid:

> NFC tag detected.

Prefer:

> Preparing your journey…

---

When the journey opens:

Avoid:

> Opening album.

Prefer:

> Reliving **Japan**…

---

# Unknown Magnet

Avoid:

> Unknown tag.

Prefer:

> This magnet hasn't been linked to a journey yet.

---

# Import Flow

Avoid:

> Select folder.

Prefer:

> Choose a journey folder.

---

Avoid:

> Import complete.

Prefer:

> Your journey is ready.

---

# Journey Completion

Avoid:

> End of slideshow.

Prefer:

> Journey complete.

---

Then gently return to the Gallery.

---

# Error Messages

Errors should explain the situation without exposing technical details.

## Example

Instead of:

> File not found.

Use:

> One or more memories couldn't be loaded.

The rest of your journey is ready to enjoy.

---

Instead of:

> Unsupported format.

Use:

> Some files couldn't be imported because they aren't supported yet.

---

Instead of:

> Storage full.

Use:

> There isn't enough space to add this journey.

Remove another journey or free some storage, then try again.

---

# Administrative Language

Administrative mode may use slightly more technical wording, but should remain approachable.

Examples:

* Add Journey
* Journey Folder
* Import Preview
* Storage Used
* Write Magnet
* Verify Magnet
* Remove Journey

Avoid engineering terminology such as:

* URI
* Database
* MIME Type
* Payload
* Media Entity

These belong in logs and documentation, not the interface.

---

# Accessibility Language

Screen reader labels should describe actions naturally.

Examples:

* Relive Journey
* Return to Gallery
* Replay Live Photo
* Pause Journey
* Resume Journey

Avoid abbreviations where possible.

---

# Guiding Principles

Every piece of text should answer one question:

> **Does this help someone remember?**

If a sentence feels technical, rewrite it.

If a word sounds like software, replace it with something more human.

If a phrase does not contribute to the feeling of reliving a journey, it probably does not belong.

---

# Brand Promise

Magnet Memories exists to preserve stories, not simply photographs.

Every interaction should reinforce one simple idea:

> **Every magnet tells a story.**
